1.] SCENARIO 1-

Answer :
In Agile, testing should begin from the beginning of the sprint, not after development is completely finished. As soon as requirements and user stories are discussed, the QA team should start reviewing them, identifying possible test scenarios, and preparing test cases. During the two-week sprint, developers and testers work together continuously. For example, if the sprint includes a “Place Food Order” feature, QA can start preparing test cases while the developers are building the feature and then test it as soon as a working build is available.

>> Entry Criteria – 2 examples

1)Requirements/User Story is clear: The user story should have clear requirements and acceptance criteria so that the tester knows what needs to be tested.

2)Testable build is available: The developed feature should be integrated and deployed to the testing environment, with the basic functionality working enough for testing to begin.

Exit Criteria – 2 examples

1)Planned testing is completed: All important test cases for the sprint have been executed, and critical/high-severity defects have been fixed or properly accepted by the team.

2)Acceptance criteria are satisfied: The user story meets its acceptance criteria, and there are no known critical issues that would prevent the feature from being released.

>> Agile Testing vs Waterfall Testing

The main difference is when and how testing is performed. In Agile, testing is a continuous activity that happens throughout every sprint. Developers and testers work closely together, and defects are found and fixed quickly within the same sprint whenever possible. 
Requirements can also change based on feedback.

In Waterfall, testing generally starts after the development phase is completed. The requirements and development phases are completed one after another, so testers usually get the complete application later in the process. If a major defect or requirement problem is found at that stage, fixing it can be more expensive and time-consuming.


